@meta
id: 03-01
title: Append at the end without going there
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-A
par: 12
minutes: 3
vscode: click at the end of the line, type the semicolon
open: retry.config.ts

@brief
Line one of retry.config.ts is missing the semicolon that every other line
has. Add it.

The cursor starts at column one, where the `e` of `export` is. In VS Code
you would click at the far end of the line first, because the cursor has to
be there before you can type there. Vim does not work that way: you can say
"start typing at the end of this line" as a single instruction, from
wherever the cursor happens to be sitting.

Do not walk the cursor along the line to get there.

@keys
| `a` | append — insert to the right of the cursor |
| `$` | move to the end of the line |
| `A` | append at the end of the line, from anywhere on it |
| `Esc` | back to normal mode |
| `:wq` | write and quit |

@hints
You already know a two-key way to do this from chapter one: get to the end
of the line, then append. There is a one-key way that does both.
--
The key is `A`. Capital letters in vim are usually the bigger, further, or
more aggressive version of the lowercase key. `a` appends here; `A` appends
at the end.
--
`A`, type the semicolon, `Esc`, then `:wq` and Enter.

@solution
    A ; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Seven keystrokes, and only one of them is the edit.

`A` is exactly `$a` in a single key. That matters more than it sounds like
it should, because appending to the end of a line is one of the most common
things you will ever do to code: a semicolon, a comma, a closing brace, a
trailing argument. Any time you catch yourself pressing `$` and then `a`,
press `A` instead.

The other half of the lesson is the column you started in. It did not
matter. A vim command that says "the end of the line" does not care where
you are on the line, so you never pay for positioning first. Half of what
makes vim fast is commands that make your current position irrelevant.

@start retry.config.ts
export const RETRY_LIMIT = 3
export const BACKOFF_MS = 500;
export const QUEUE_NAME = 'payments.refunds';

@expected retry.config.ts
export const RETRY_LIMIT = 3;
export const BACKOFF_MS = 500;
export const QUEUE_NAME = 'payments.refunds';
